      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We begin by analyzing the function $G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|$. To do this, we consider the different cases:\n$(1)$ If $(x < -1)$, we substitute $x < -1$ into the function and get $G(x) = (-x-1) - (x-1) = -2x$.\n$(2)$ If $-1 \\leq x < 1$, we substitute $-1 \\leq x < 1$ into the function and get $G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = 2$.\n$(3)$ If $x \\geq 1$, we substitute $x \\geq 1$ into the function and get $G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = 2$.\nWe can see the function $G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|$ takes on the values $-2x$ for $x < -1$, takes on the value $2$ for $-1 \\leq x < 1$ and $2$ for $x \\geq 1$. Since the values are monotonic above and below zero, $G(x)$ has a range of $(-\\infty, 2]$\\boxed{}.",
